Revision history for File-SOPS

{{$NEXT}}
  IMPORTANT - if you encrypted data with 0.001 or 0.002, read this.
  The defects below could corrupt or misrepresent stored values, and
  two of them are not repairable from the existing ciphertext:
    * A false boolean was written as an unencrypted empty value. Such a
      document also fails its own MAC check, so this release may refuse
      to decrypt it at all - and the original value is gone, replaced by
      a placeholder indistinguishable from an empty string.
    * The string 'false' was encrypted as True - a silent value
      inversion. It decrypts without error and returns the wrong value.
    * No YAML file written by 0.001 or 0.002 could be read by the sops
      CLI. Those files are intact and are readable again once
      re-encrypted with this version.
    * A document with a key in the Latin-1 range (a key containing e.g.
      é or ö) was written with a Latin-1 AAD where sops uses UTF-8.
      Neither sops nor this release can decrypt such a value; the
      ciphertext is intact but recovering it needs the old AAD. Keys
      above U+00FF could not be written at all - they died. Values
      under ASCII keys are unaffected.
    * A VALUE in the Latin-1 range that was passed as an unflagged Perl
      string was written to the wire as Latin-1 where sops uses UTF-8.
      Such documents still decrypt and still verify under this release -
      the ciphertext is intact and the MAC covers those same bytes - but
      the value comes back as bytes rather than characters, and sops
      reads it as a binary scalar rather than as the text you stored.
      Re-encrypting with this release writes it correctly. The same
      value under unencrypted_suffix produced a document that failed
      verification when it was written; there is nothing to recover
      there, and it must be rewritten from the plaintext.
    * encrypt_file on a file that was ALREADY encrypted encrypted every
      value a second time and wrote the result, silently - and with
      output defaulting to input, over the original. THE VALUES ARE
      GONE. Parsing split the existing sops section off and the new one
      replaced it, so the file that was written holds the inner
      ENC[...] strings under a NEW data key while the data key they
      were encrypted with is not in the file at all. Decrypting such a
      file returns the inner ENC[...] strings; nothing can decrypt
      those. If you have a copy of the file from before it was
      re-encrypted, its sops section holds the missing key.
    * A top-level key literally named 'sops' in the data you encrypted
      was overwritten by the metadata section. The value is gone, and
      the document fails its own MAC because the digest covered it.
    * A null value was written as an empty string, so a key whose value
      was null came back as '' and reached sops as '' too. Repairable
      by re-encrypting from the plaintext.
  Re-encrypt affected documents from their ORIGINAL PLAINTEXT, not from
  the stored ciphertext. Where a value gated access or behaviour, verify
  it by hand: a wrong value may have been in effect since it was written.

  - Fix Perl-encrypted YAML being rejected outright by the sops CLI:
    lastmodified was emitted as a bare RFC3339 scalar, which Go's YAML
    parser types as time.Time where sops expects a string, so sops
    refused the file before decrypting anything. It is now emitted
    quoted, as sops itself writes it. JSON was never affected. This
    means no YAML file written by 0.001 or 0.002 could be read by the
    sops CLI; such files are readable again after re-encrypting with
    this version.
  - Fix false booleans being written as unencrypted empty values:
    JSON::PP::Boolean overloads eq, so JSON->false eq '' is true and
    the empty-value check swallowed them. Affected documents also
    failed their own MAC verification on read-back.
  - Fix the string 'false' being encrypted as True, caused by a bare
    Perl truthiness fallback in both boolean serializers.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - a value's type now comes from the value, not from
    a pattern match on its text. sops types a value by what the YAML or
    JSON parser returned, so a quoted "false", "true", "1", "0", "007"
    or "1.50" is type:str while a bare false is type:bool; File::SOPS
    matched the text instead, so it returned the string "true" as a
    boolean and "007" as the integer 7, silently changing values it
    exists to preserve. In the other direction it wrote "007" as
    type:int and "1.50" as type:float with the source spelling as the
    plaintext, and since Go recomputes the MAC from the canonical form
    (7 and 1.5) the sops CLI refused such files outright. What changes
    for callers: a Perl string that reads like a number or a boolean is
    now written as type:str and comes back as that string, and a Perl
    number is written in Go's canonical form (1.50 as 1.5, 1e20 as
    100000000000000000000). type:bool now requires JSON->true /
    JSON->false or a true/false loaded from YAML or JSON, because Perl
    has no native boolean. Existing encrypted documents are unaffected;
    only what this release writes, and what it gives back, moves. The
    reasoning, the measurements behind it and the one case where Perl
    can be misled are recorded in docs/adr/0002.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - values are UTF-8 encoded unconditionally, like
    the AAD, instead of only when the scalar carries Perl's UTF-8 flag.
    Below U+0100 that flag is storage and not meaning - "caf\x{e9}" may
    be held as one byte or as two and Perl considers both the same
    string - but both serializers write it to the file as UTF-8 either
    way. So a value that reached the wire as Latin-1 disagreed with our
    own emitter: with unencrypted_suffix, on by default, the document
    failed its own MAC and sops reported "MAC mismatch"; when the value
    was encrypted the document was self-consistent but sops -d handed
    the value back as "!!binary Y2Fm6Q==" instead of "café". What
    changes for callers: passing UTF-8 BYTES to encrypt now
    double-encodes them, so decode at your own boundary first. That
    appeared to work before only for encrypted values - the emitter was
    already double-encoding such a caller's unencrypted ones, and those
    documents already failed verification. type:bytes is exempt on both
    sides and is how a caller says a scalar really is bytes. Recorded in
    docs/adr/0003.
  - New File::SOPS::Encrypted class methods detect_type and
    value_to_bytes, the single source of truth for the value-to-wire
    mapping. The type ladder and the value conversion previously existed
    twice, once for the ciphertext and once for the MAC digest, and had
    to be kept byte-identical by hand - when they drifted both were
    consistently wrong and only the sops binary noticed.
  - Fix compilation when JSON::PP has not been loaded by something else
    first: _deserialize_value used JSON::PP::true/false as barewords
    behind a runtime require, so File::SOPS failed to compile on its own
    (JSON::MaybeXS does not pull in JSON::PP when Cpanel::JSON::XS is
    installed). Now uses JSON->true/JSON->false via JSON::MaybeXS.
  - Guard against dying on unblessed references reaching the value
    serializers, and route all boolean handling through JSON::MaybeXS.
  - SECURITY: decrypt no longer returns data it could not verify. A
    missing mac, a mac that is not a well-formed ENC value, and a mac
    that will not decrypt under the data key and the document's
    lastmodified all silently disabled verification and returned the
    document as though it had been checked. All three now die, matching
    sops, which reports "File has no MAC" / "Cannot decrypt MAC" and
    stops. Pass ignore_mac => 1 to decrypt, decrypt_file, extract or
    rotate for the old behaviour; what it returns is decrypted but not
    authenticated.
  - Fix MAC verification of any document containing a value excluded
    from encryption. sops covers unencrypted values in the MAC on both
    sides; File::SOPS hashed them when encrypting and skipped them when
    decrypting, so with the default unencrypted_suffix of _unencrypted
    it rejected its own output and could not read any sops file with
    such a key. No configuration was needed to hit it.
  - Fix MAC verification of values Perl's numeric conversion does not
    round-trip. The decrypt side hashed the value after int() / + 0.0,
    so '007' was hashed as 007 when written and 7 when read, and the
    expanded 100000000000000000000 that sops writes for 1e20 came back
    as 1e+20. It now hashes the authenticated plaintext.
  - Fix MAC verification of documents with a key ending in "mac". The
    skip that kept the metadata MAC out of its own digest matched any
    key containing "mac:", so hmac and webmac values were dropped from
    the digest. The metadata MAC is now excluded structurally, by
    dropping the sops branch, and the pattern match is gone.
  - Honour mac_only_encrypted, including the fixed initialization block
    sops uses to keep the two settings' digests apart, and accept it as
    an option to encrypt / encrypt_file. rotate carries it over.
  - New File::SOPS::Encrypted method decrypt_bytes, returning the
    authenticated plaintext without type conversion, and new
    File::SOPS::Metadata method should_encrypt_path.
  - MAC verification now recovers document key order from an
    order-preserving reparse (YAML::PP, new prerequisite) instead of
    scraping ENC values out of the raw text, which is what makes
    unencrypted values placeable at all. YAML::XS and JSON::MaybeXS
    remain the parsers and emitters.
  - Fix any document with a non-ASCII key. The AAD is built from the key
    path and was handed to AES-GCM as a character string, so a key above
    U+00FF died with "Wide character in subroutine entry" and a key in
    the Latin-1 range was quietly authenticated as Latin-1 where sops
    used UTF-8 - a file that looks correct and that neither sops nor
    this library can read back. The AAD is now always UTF-8, matching
    both sops and the bytes our own emitters write the key as. Affects
    reading and writing, and the MAC, which derives the same AAD.
  - Fix decrypted values coming back as UTF-8 bytes where encrypt took
    characters. A decrypted structure compared unequal to the one that
    was encrypted, and decrypt_file encoded those bytes a second time,
    so a non-ASCII file round-tripped through encrypt_file/decrypt_file
    came back as mojibake. decrypt, decrypt_file and extract now return
    characters. The bytes written to the file are unchanged. See the new
    "Character encoding" section in File::SOPS for the boundary rule.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - the top-level 'sops' key is reserved, and both
    ways of colliding with it now die instead of succeeding silently.
    encrypt refuses a data structure carrying that key, because
    serialization writes the metadata there and the user's value was
    simply overwritten - after the digest had already covered it, so the
    document failed its own MAC on the next read. encrypt_file refuses
    an input that already has a sops section, because parsing split it
    off before encrypt ever saw it and every ENC[...] string was then
    encrypted a second time under a NEW data key, while the section
    holding the key they were encrypted with was discarded rather than
    written back - so the values became unrecoverable, and with output
    defaulting to input that happened over the original. Measured: sops
    makes no distinction between
    the two either, and refuses both with exit code 203 and the same
    message. Use rotate to re-key an encrypted file. The format
    handlers' serialize method refuses the same collision.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - an integer outside Go's int64 range is refused
    rather than written. Perl's integers reach 2**64-1 and Go's int
    stops at 2**63-1, and there is no wire form that preserves the
    difference: written as type:int, sops -d stops with "strconv.Atoi:
    value out of range" (exit 25); unencrypted, it stopped the YAML walk
    outright ("unknown type: uint64") or produced a MAC mismatch in
    JSON, because Go re-derived the digest from the float64 it parsed.
    sops's own JSON store silently truncates such an integer to a
    float64 and its YAML store refuses the document; this release
    refuses in both, and says so at the key path. Store the digits as a
    STRING to keep them - that is type:str, written verbatim, and it
    survives both implementations. Reading is deliberately unaffected
    for values sops legitimately writes: its truncated 12345678901234567000
    parses back as a Perl integer above int64 and must still hash as
    those digits. Reading a type:int whose plaintext is out of range, or
    is not an integer at all, now dies as Go does; decrypt_bytes still
    returns the plaintext, which is how such a value is recovered.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - an unknown type: field is now an error ("Unknown
    datatype", as Go says) instead of quietly returning the raw
    plaintext, so a document this library cannot interpret no longer
    looks like it was read. type:time and type:comment are added to the
    ladder rather than rejected: sops emits both - a bare RFC3339 scalar
    or date becomes type:time, and a YAML comment becomes a type:comment
    node written as #ENC[...] on its own line - and both already worked
    by accident through the unknown branch. A type:time value is
    returned as its RFC3339 string, since Perl has no native date. A
    type:float plaintext that is not a number is an error too, where
    + 0.0 turned it into 0 without a word.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - base64 in an ENC value is now decoded strictly.
    MIME::Base64 ignores characters outside the alphabet and stops at a
    short group, so a corrupted data, iv or tag field decoded to
    something shorter and surfaced much later as "Authentication failed
    - data may be corrupted", or as a cipher rejecting the nonce length.
    Go fails immediately, and names the field. is_encrypted is
    unaffected and still never dies: it answers about the shape.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - encrypt_value refuses an empty value. GCM
    ciphertext is as long as its plaintext, so an empty one produced
    ENC[AES256_GCM,data:,...] - a string neither this module's own regex
    nor sops accepts ("Input string ENC[...] does not match sops' data
    format", exit 25). sops leaves empty values unencrypted, which is
    what File::SOPS::encrypt does before it ever calls encrypt_value.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - extract dies on a path that does not exist,
    naming the component that was not found. A missing TOP-LEVEL key
    used to return undef while a missing nested one died, so the same
    mistake was silent or loud depending on where it was made - and
    undef was indistinguishable from a key whose value really is null.
    sops reports "component ['nope'] not found" at every level.
  - Fix extract's bracket notation with an array index. The parser only
    recognised double-quoted components, so the documented ["items"][0]
    matched "items" alone and returned the whole ArrayRef instead of the
    element. Bare indices and single-quoted keys now work, as they do in
    sops --extract. Its POD claimed extract was more efficient than
    decrypting the whole file (it decrypts the whole file) and that it
    returned a scalar and not a reference (extracting a branch returns
    the branch, as sops does); both claims are corrected.
  - Fix undef being written as an empty string. sops leaves a null alone
    in YAML and in JSON alike, so a key whose value was null came back
    from File::SOPS as '' and reached sops as '' too. Both hash as
    nothing, so the digest never noticed and the document verified
    either way - the value was simply not the one that went in.
  - Fix $YAML::XS::Boolean being set at load time. It is a process
    global that changes what YAML::XS::Load and ::Dump do for every
    other user of them in the same interpreter, so merely loading
    File::SOPS rewrote how unrelated code parsed YAML. It is now set
    with local around this distribution's own calls. decrypt_file, which
    calls YAML::XS::Dump directly, was depending on that global to avoid
    emitting booleans as !!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean, and now sets
    it for itself.
  - Failures say where and why. A value that would not parse, decrypt or
    place was skipped during the MAC walk, so the digest quietly covered
    a different document than the one on disk and the only symptom was
    "MAC verification failed" with no indication of the culprit - which
    is what made every other MAC defect in this distribution expensive
    to find. Value errors now name the key path on both the encrypt and
    the decrypt side, a structural disagreement between the document and
    the parsed tree is reported at the path it happens at instead of
    truncating the digest, and the MAC message says how many leaves were
    covered, in which order, and how to read the file anyway. No error
    message contains a plaintext value, a data key or an age identity -
    the key path only, which a SOPS document leaves readable by design.
    The digests themselves are deliberately not printed, unlike sops:
    a SHA-512 over the concatenated plaintexts is an oracle for a
    document with one short secret in it.
  - Fix multi-document YAML being silently reduced to its last document.
    The stream was loaded in scalar context, which returns only that one,
    so encrypting a two-document file wrote a single document back and
    discarded the rest without an error - data loss on a write path, and
    unrecoverable when encrypting in place. Such input is now refused.
    sops does support multi-document YAML, but as one tree of several
    branches: one metadata section copied into every document and one MAC
    spanning all of them in order. Matching that is a data-model change
    and is not in this release, so the file is rejected rather than
    truncated, and reading a multi-document sops file now says so instead
    of reporting a MAC mismatch. The measured behaviour of the reference
    implementation is recorded under "Multi-document YAML" in
    File::SOPS::Format::YAML. The MAC's order-preserving reparse now holds
    the same one-document rule on its own: YAML::PP and YAML::XS pick
    opposite ends of a multi-document stream in scalar context, so a
    reparse that accepted one would have taken its key order from a
    different document than its values.
  - Document how a caller says what a value is, in the new "Saying what
    a value is" section of File::SOPS. A scalar read in numeric context
    is retyped in place, so `if ($cfg->{port} > 1024)` before
    encrypting writes type:int; reading a scalar numerically leaves its
    string intact, so $cfg->{port} = "$cfg->{port}" restores both the
    type and the original text, and 0 + $x / 0.0 + $x ask for the other
    direction. No per-leaf type argument was added to encrypt: the
    scalar is the channel, and the one case the idiom cannot fix (a
    numeric ASSIGNMENT, which replaces the string before this module
    sees it) is one no argument could fix either.
  - Fix the encryption rules being applied one level at a time instead
    of to a value's whole key path. sops encrypts a leaf as soon as
    SOME component of its path matches, and the tree walk asked about
    each key as it descended - right for unencrypted_suffix and
    unencrypted_regex, where an excluded branch stays excluded anyway,
    and wrong for encrypted_suffix and encrypted_regex: everything
    under an `api_enc:` block was left readable, and a `token_enc:`
    under an ordinary parent was never reached because the walk stopped
    descending there. The resulting file was one sops refused. Latent
    until now only because no rule could be configured at all.
  - SECURITY: rotate no longer silently discards the parts of the sops
    section it did not create. It re-encrypted through encrypt, which
    built fresh metadata with the defaults, so a rotated file lost its
    encryption rules and every backend's key material except age -
    which survived only because rotate re-read the age recipients by
    hand. Rotating a file shared with PGP or KMS recipients therefore
    revoked their access while reporting success, and the file kept
    decrypting perfectly for whoever ran the command, so nothing looked
    wrong until someone else needed it. rotate now carries the
    document's rules, mac_only_encrypted and unmodelled fields across,
    and REFUSES a file holding key material it cannot re-encrypt (pgp,
    kms, gcp_kms, azure_kv, hc_vault, key_groups) rather than dropping
    it: a new data key makes those entries stale whatever is done with
    them. Rotate such a file with the sops CLI, or drop those
    recipients deliberately with decrypt followed by encrypt.
  - rotate also dies with a clear message on a file that has no sops
    section, instead of on an undefined method call.
  - New File::SOPS::Metadata method key_material_fields.
  - The encryption rules are reachable at last. unencrypted_suffix,
    encrypted_suffix, unencrypted_regex and encrypted_regex have been
    documented since 0.001 and could not be set through the public API:
    encrypt always built its metadata with the defaults, so the only
    rule that could ever be in effect was the _unencrypted default.
    All four are now arguments to encrypt and encrypt_file, and a whole
    policy can be carried over from an existing document with
    metadata => $meta. Pass unencrypted_suffix => undef for a document
    with no rule at all.
  - The four rules are mutually exclusive, and setting two now dies
    here instead of producing a file sops refuses to open ("Cannot use
    more than one of encrypted_suffix, unencrypted_suffix, ..."). The
    _unencrypted default stands down as soon as another rule is set,
    which is what sops does when creating a document.
  - Metadata no longer drops the fields of the sops section it does not
    model. shamir_threshold, key_groups and anything a later sops adds
    are preserved verbatim across a rewrite, in the new extra
    attribute. sops preserves the fields it knows and drops the rest;
    preserving everything is a superset of that and a safe one, since
    it ignores what it does not recognise.
  - New File::SOPS::Metadata attribute extra and methods policy_args
    and rule_value.
  - Tests: the interop suite is no longer the only compatibility
    coverage. New regression tests pin the wire format, the serializers'
    key ordering, every MAC rule above and the encoding boundary without
    requiring a sops binary, which is what let the above ship unnoticed.
    The encoding tests include a document written by the real sops
    3.13.3, checked in with its throwaway age identity, so the read side
    is pinned against the reference implementation even when the binary
    is absent. Four more files pin the reserved sops key, the integer
    range, the API edges above and the diagnostics, and the interop
    suite gained the Go half of each: the exit-203 refusal, sops's own
    int64 boundary in both formats, nulls in both directions, and a real
    sops document containing timestamps and comments.

0.002     2026-02-22 02:10:34Z
  - Fix bool deserialization: return JSON::PP::Boolean instead of plain 1/0
    (YAML::XS with $Boolean='JSON::PP' now correctly serializes true/false)

0.001     2026-01-11 04:00:35Z
  - Initial release
  - Pure Perl implementation of Mozilla SOPS encrypted file format
  - Full interoperability with sops CLI (tested with v3.9.4)
  - age encryption backend via Crypt::Age
  - AES-256-GCM value encryption with type preservation (str, int, float, bool)
  - YAML and JSON format support
  - MAC verification for tamper detection
  - Multiple recipient support
  - Key rotation support
  - File encrypt/decrypt operations
  - Single value extraction via path notation
  - Comprehensive test suite including CLI interop tests
